# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-wendyos-data.rules
#
# Stable alias /dev/wendyos/data for the persistent /data device.
#
# /data is a plain partition on some devices and an unlocked LUKS mapper on
# others. Once encryption is a runtime decision, the image cannot know which
# one it will be at build time. A .mount unit's What= is a fixed string, so
# the device path is what has to vary. The alias is that varying part.
#
# It has to be a udev SYMLINK, not a hand-made "ln -s" under /dev. systemd
# derives a .device unit from every udev symlink, so /dev/wendyos/data becomes
# dev-wendyos-data.device and a mount unit can wait on it. A plain symlink has
# no unit behind it, and the mount would hang waiting for a .device unit that
# never activates.
#
# The 99 prefix is load-bearing. These rules match properties that other rules
# have to import first: 60-persistent-storage.rules runs blkid and sets
# ID_PART_ENTRY_SCHEME, ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME and ID_FS_TYPE, and the
# device-mapper rules set DM_NAME. Sorting any earlier would match on values
# that are not there yet.
#
# This file covers the two cases every board can hit:
#   1. GPT boards, plaintext /data. Matched by the GPT partition name.
#   2. Any board, encrypted /data. Matched by the device-mapper name.
#
# Rule 1 excludes crypto_LUKS so it stops matching the moment the partition is
# LUKS-formatted. From then on only rule 2 provides the alias. The encrypted
# case has to be matched by mapper name because luksFormat also destroys the
# ext4 label, so there is no "data" label left to match.
#
# The MBR case cannot be matched by partition name, because MBR has no
# partition names. It lives in the companion file 99-wendyos-data-mbr.rules,
# which is installed only on the one board with an MBR layout. See that file
# for why it is not shipped fleet-wide.
#
# As of this change nothing consumes /dev/wendyos/data yet.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_SCHEME}=="gpt", \
    ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME}=="data", ENV{ID_FS_TYPE}!="crypto_LUKS", \
    SYMLINK+="wendyos/data"

ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="dm-*", ENV{DM_NAME}=="data", \
    SYMLINK+="wendyos/data"
